course: language
lesson: 17
title: Slang and how it changes with every generation
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Slang is the new, playful, in-group language of a time and a crowd; every generation makes its own, partly so it can sound different from its parents.
explain: Picture one pizza and three generations: Grandad says "That's groovy!" (1960s), Mum says "That's cool!" (an old jazz word from the 1930s and 1940s that never died), and Mia says "That's bussin'!" (today, meaning really tasty).
explain: Slang moves fast: when parents start saying "sick" or "lit", young people often drop it and invent something new; but some slang settles down and ends up in dictionaries, like "rizz" (charm), Oxford's Word of the Year for 2023.
explain: Slang is often silly taken word by word: "the bee's knees" (1920s, excellent), "don't have a cow" (calm down), "skibidi" (often just a fun nonsense word); you read the whole phrase, not each word.
explain: Ordinary words drift too: "nice" once meant foolish, and today's slang might be tomorrow's plain word; slang also differs by place, from London's "peng" to Canada's "toque".
fact: Every generation makes its own slang; groovy (great) was big in the 1960s, cool goes back to jazz slang of the 1930s and 1940s, and bussin' (really tasty, excellent) is young slang today, from African American English.
fact: Rizz comes from "charisma" and means charm, the knack of getting people to like you; it was the Oxford Word of the Year for 2023.
fact: "No cap" means no lie, for real; in this slang a "cap" is a lie.
fact: Skibidi comes from a 2023 YouTube animated series; kids use it as a fun word that can mean cool, bad or nothing, and it was added to the Cambridge Dictionary in 2025.
fact: "Don't have a cow" is American slang for "don't get so upset"; it became famous through Bart Simpson in the 1990s.
fact: "The bee's knees" and "the cat's pyjamas" meant something excellent in 1920s American slang; they were playful nonsense.
fact: "Sick" meaning great became common in the 1980s and 1990s; "cringe" means embarrassing.
fact: "Lowkey" means a bit or secretly; "highkey" means very or openly.
fact: In Canada a "double-double" is a coffee with two creams and two sugars, and a "toque" is a knitted woolly hat.
fact: "Nice" comes from Latin "nescius", not knowing; in Middle English it meant silly or foolish, later shy, then fussy, and now pleasant.
fact: In London youth slang "peng" means great or good-looking and "bare" means very or lots; in parts of the North "mint" means great and "aye" means yes.
fact: "Rad", short for radical, was 1970s and 1980s American surfer and skater slang for excellent; the ordinary word radical means a very big or extreme change.
exercise: Grandad says "groovy", Mum says "cool", Mia says "bussin'" about the same pizza. What does this show? | Each generation has its own slang for the same idea | Only Mia's word is real English, the others are mistakes | They all disagree about whether the pizza is any good | Grandad and Mum are using words from another language
exercise: Which slang word is the oldest still in use? | cool | bussin' | rizz | skibidi
exercise: What does "rizz" mean? | Charm, the knack of getting people to like you | A kind of sweet fizzy drink sold in American corner shops | Being very angry and shouting at someone | A dance move from a video game
exercise: Jacob's cousin says "no cap". What does it mean? | No lie, I'm being honest, for real | No hat allowed in the house | No cap on the bottle, so it will spill | No limit to how much you can have
exercise: Year 4 kids shout "Skibidi!" Where did the word come from? | A 2023 YouTube animated series | An old Shakespeare play about a king | A Scottish word for a small dog | A Latin word meaning "jump up and down"
exercise: Does skibidi always have one clear meaning? | No, kids use it for fun with many meanings | Yes, it always means "cool" and nothing else | Yes, it always means "bad" and nothing else | Yes, it is the name of a famous footballer
exercise: Nana says "Don't have a cow!" What does she mean? | Calm down, there's no need to get so upset | Don't eat any beef for dinner tonight | Don't bring any animals into the kitchen | Don't buy a cow at the farmers' market
exercise: In an old 1920s film someone is "the bee's knees". What does it mean? | Excellent | Very small and quick, like an insect | Clumsy, always falling over things | Stung by a bee on the leg
exercise: Isaac's dad says "That's sick!" and Isaac groans. Why does slang change so fast? | Each generation wants its own words, so they drop words parents use | The government changes the official list of allowed slang every single year | Dictionaries ban slang words after five years | Slang words wear out like old shoes and fall apart
exercise: A teenager says "She's lowkey annoyed but highkey hungry." What does she mean? | A bit annoyed but very hungry | Very annoyed but only a little hungry | Annoyed in a quiet voice and hungry in a loud one | Annoyed downstairs and hungry upstairs
exercise: In Toronto, Jay asks for "a double-double and a toque". What does he want? | A coffee with two creams and two sugars, and a woolly hat | Two big burgers and a cold fizzy drink with ice | Two tickets for a hockey match and a scarf | Double helpings of chips and a paper napkin
exercise: What did "nice" mean in Middle English? | Silly or foolish | Kind and friendly, just like today | Very cold, like winter weather | Expensive and hard to buy
exercise: A Hackney girl says the trainers are "peng"; her Leeds cousin says they're "mint". Do they disagree? | No, both words mean great, so they agree | Yes, peng means ugly and mint means great | Yes, mint means they smell of mint | No, both words mean too expensive to buy
exercise: Uncle Ray says "rad" in his day. What did it mean? | Excellent | Angry and red in the face | Very fast on a skateboard | Strange and a bit scary
exercise: Do slang words ever get into dictionaries? | Yes, popular ones often do, like rizz | No, dictionaries never include any slang words | Only if the King agrees to it first | Only after a hundred years of use
exercise: What does "cringe" mean in slang? | Embarrassing | Brave and strong | Tasty and crunchy | Fast and exciting
test: Your mate goes "old people's slang is cringe, ours is the best, no cap." What's the fair view? | Every generation's slang felt cool to them; ours will sound old one day too | He's right, slang from today will be cool forever and never change | Old slang had no meaning, only today's slang means something | Slang was only invented when phones came along
test: Your grandad, a proper boomer, says "Bussin'? Sounds like a bus." Your sister explains. What does she say? | It means really tasty or excellent food | It means riding on a bus with friends | It means being late for the school bus | It means kissing someone on the cheek
test: What would happen if no new slang were ever made? | Language would change more slowly and lose one of its playful sides | Nothing at all, because slang has never changed English | Everyone would forget how to talk within a year | All the old slang would become illegal
test: A Kiwi uncle texts "sweet as, the barbie's on this arvo" ("barbie" means barbecue). Your Gen Alpha cousin replies "bussin'". Do they understand each other? | Yes, both are happy: great, barbecue this afternoon, sounds tasty | No, sweet as means the food is too sugary | No, bussin' means he's taking the bus instead | Yes, they both say the doll is coming round
test: Grandma uses "nice" for everything. A friend says "nice has always meant pleasant." What's true? | Long ago it meant foolish; meanings drift over time | It has always meant pleasant since the Romans | It meant pleasant in Latin and English never changed it | It is a brand-new word from the 1990s
test: In a cartoon, a cowboy says "Don't have a cow!" and a real cow walks off. Why is that a joke? | The slang means calm down, not owning a cow | Cowboys really were banned from having cows | The cow understood English and felt hurt | It's not a joke, the phrase is about farming
test: Your teacher says "skibidi" can't be a real word "cos it means nothing." What's interesting about that? | Some slang is mainly about belonging to a group, and it's now in a dictionary | She's right, a word must always have one clear meaning or it doesn't count | It means "toilet" in Latin, so it does mean something | It is a real word only in Scotland
test: Your dad says "That trick was sick!" Isaac says "Dad, nobody says sick anymore." Why did Isaac groan? | Kids often drop slang once parents start using it | Sick only means ill, so his dad used the word wrongly | Sick is a very rude word in slang | Dads are not allowed to use slang by law
test: A Canadian kid says "Grab your toque, eh, it's freezing." What should you grab? | A woolly hat | A hot drink | A hockey stick | A warm coat
test: Your brother says "highkey this lesson is bare long." ("Bare" means very.) What does he mean? | This lesson is really, very long | This lesson is quietly a bit short | This lesson is long but very fun | This lesson is in a high classroom
test: A 1920s film says a dancer is "the cat's pyjamas". What would a kid say today? | She's amazing, she ate that | She's sleepy and needs her bed | She's wearing cat-patterned clothes | She's scared of cats and runs away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon of slang? | Informal vocabulary of a particular group or period, subject to rapid change | Vocabulary prohibited from use in every circumstance by schools and courts | Vocabulary of fixed meaning which never alters over time | The technical language of scientists and lawyers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which slang terms are frequently abandoned by younger speakers. | Their adoption by older speakers diminishes their value as markers of a group | Their prohibition by national dictionaries after a fixed period | Their physical deterioration through excessive use | Their replacement by official decree each year
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the word "rizz". | It derives from "charisma" and was selected as Oxford Word of the Year for 2023 | It derives from a Latin verb meaning to laugh loudly at a joke or at a friend | It has never been recorded in any dictionary of English | It denotes a beverage originating in Canada
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the word "nice" in Middle English. | Foolish | Pleasant and agreeable | Of considerable expense | Of exceptionally low temperature
